<body>

<!-- ══════════════ HEADER ══════════════ -->
<header>
  <span class="oscar-icon">🏆</span>
  <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A Historical Trend Analysis of the Academy Award for Best Original Score, 1934–2026</p>
  <div class="report-meta">
    <span>93 Ceremonies</span>
    <span>1934–2026</span>
    <span>69 Unique Composers</span>
    <span>Analytics Report · April 2026</span>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

<!-- ══════════════ NAV ══════════════ -->
<nav>
  <h3>Contents</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#overview">Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</a></li>
    <li><a href="#kpis">Key Performance Indicators</a></li>
    <li><a href="#top-composers">Most Awarded Composers</a></li>
    <li><a href="#temporal">Temporal Patterns &amp; Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</a></li>
    <li><a href="#era">Era-Based Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#milestones">Notable Milestones &amp; Records</a></li>
    <li><a href="#conclusions">Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</a></li>
    <li><a href="#fulldata">Complete Dataset (1934–2026)</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<!-- ══════════════ 1. OVERVIEW ══════════════ -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2><span class="sec-num">01</span> Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>

  <p>
    The Academy Award for Best Original Score is among Hollywood's most storied honors — and one of the few categories
    that has tracked the full arc of the film industry, from the nascent talkies of the 1930s to the globally
    diverse cinema of today. This report analyzes all <strong>93 winners</strong> from the 6th Academy Awards
    ceremony (1934) through the 98th ceremony in March 2026, a span that encompasses virtually every era of
    cinematic and musical history.
  </p>
  <p>
    The dataset contains three columns — <code>Year</code>, <code>Film</code>, and <code>Composer</code> — and
    is complete with no missing values. Composer names have been standardized for consistent counting; in years
    where multiple composers shared the award, they appear together as a single entry (e.g., 1939's
    <em>Stagecoach</em>, credited to four co-composers).
  </p>
  <p>
    The award's history is not without complexity: the Academy has at various points split the category into
    separate dramatic and comedy/musical subcategories. This dataset consolidates to one winner per ceremony year,
    providing the most analytically tractable view of the category's history.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    "Nine decades of Oscar scoring history is, at its core, a story about a handful of extraordinary talents
    who found ways to dominate — and about the ever-widening global pool of composers who have challenged them."
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 2. KPIs ══════════════ -->
<section id="kpis">
  <h2><span class="sec-num">02</span> Key Performance Indicators</h2>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">69</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Unique Composers</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Across 93 ceremonies (1934–2026)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">4</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Maximum Wins by One Composer</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Achieved by 3 composers: John Williams, John Barry &amp; Ludwig Göransson</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">25.8%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Repeat Winner Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">24 of 93 ceremonies won by a composer who had already won before</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">1980s</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Decade with Most Unique Winners</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">10 distinct composers in 10 ceremonies — maximum possible diversity</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    These four statistics tell a compact but revealing story. With 69 unique composers sharing 93 wins, the
    average "winner" won roughly 1.35 times — but the distribution is sharply right-skewed, with a small
    cohort of four-time winners towering above the field. The 25.8% repeat winner rate means that
    <em>roughly one in four ceremonies</em> rewarded a composer the Academy had already honored — a pattern
    that intensifies in certain eras and relaxes in others.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 3. TOP COMPOSERS ══════════════ -->
<section id="top-composers">
  <h2><span class="sec-num">03</span> Most Awarded Composers</h2>

  <p>
    Three composers stand at the summit with four wins apiece — a figure matched by no one else in the award's
    history. What makes the current picture historically remarkable is that one of those three, Ludwig Göransson,
    has achieved this milestone in just eight years (2018–2026), a pace of accumulation unprecedented in the
    category's modern era.
  </p>

  <!-- Horizontal bar chart SVG -->
  <div class="chart-container">
    <div class="chart-title">Wins by Composer — All Multiple Winners</div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 700 340" width="100%" aria-label="Bar chart of wins by composer">
      <!-- Grid lines -->
      <line x1="180" y1="20" x2="180" y2="310" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="270" y1="20" x2="270" y2="310" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.05)" stroke-width="1"/>
