  <h3>The Big Three: Williams, Barry, and Göransson</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>John Williams</strong> (wins: 1975 <em>Jaws</em>, 1977 <em>Star Wars</em>, 1982 <em>E.T.</em>,
    1993 <em>Schindler's List</em>) defined orchestral Hollywood scoring across four decades. His wins span
    18 years — a testament to sustained excellence rather than a concentrated burst.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>John Barry</strong> (wins: 1966 <em>Born Free</em>, 1968 <em>The Lion in Winter</em>,
    1985 <em>Out of Africa</em>, 1990 <em>Dances with Wolves</em>) is the most temporally dispersed
    four-time winner — his last two wins came 17 and 22 years after his first, a record for longevity among
    the category's elite.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> (wins: 2018 <em>Black Panther</em>, 2023 <em>Oppenheimer</em>,
    2024 <em>Oppenheimer</em>, 2026 <em>Sinners</em>) is in a category of his own as the only composer
    to match the all-time wins record while still in mid-career. His four wins in eight years represent the
    fastest rate of accumulation in Oscar scoring history.
  </p>

  <h3>The Three-Win Club</h3>
  <p>
    Four composers hold three wins: <strong>Max Steiner</strong> (1935, 1943, 1945 — concentrated in the
    Golden Age), <strong>Dimitri Tiomkin</strong> (1952, 1954, 1958 — three wins in seven years),
    <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> (1962, 1965, 1984 — spanning 22 years), and <strong>Alan Menken</strong>
    (1989, 1991, 1992 — including back-to-back wins). Menken's three wins in four years during Disney's
    animation renaissance remains one of the most concentrated dominations in the award's history.
  </p>

