  <h3>Era Narratives</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Golden Age (1934–1959):</strong> The studio system produced a cohort of house composers who
    wrote in a shared idiom of sweeping Romantic orchestration. The biggest names — Steiner, Tiomkin, Waxman,
    Newman, Korngold — were staff composers at major studios, and their dominance reflects institutional power
    as much as individual genius. Tiomkin's three wins in seven years stand as the era's most concentrated run.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Silver Age (1960–1979):</strong> New Hollywood brought international influence and tonal
    experimentation. Maurice Jarre's spare, exotic scoring for David Lean epics (<em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>,
    <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>) signaled a new sophistication. John Williams emerged in 1975–1977 with two
    blockbuster wins that would define the orchestral language of popular cinema for decades. Meanwhile,
    Giorgio Moroder's synth-driven <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978) hinted at the electronic
    experimentation to come.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Modern (1980–1999):</strong> Paradoxically, this era — home to the most diverse decade (1980s) —
    also produced the tightest concentrated run of the pre-2020s era: Alan Menken's three wins in four years
    for Disney animated films. The broader picture was a genre explosion: Vangelis (electronic, 1981),
    Herbie Hancock (jazz, 1986), a three-composer collective for <em>The Last Emperor</em> (1987),
    and Jorge Moroder's predecessors — the Modern era resisted a single aesthetic center.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Contemporary (2000–2026):</strong> The most globally diverse era yet, with winners from
    Argentina (Santaolalla), Iceland (Guðnadóttir), Germany (Bertelmann), and Sweden (Göransson).
    The hybrid approach — blending electronics, world music, and orchestral elements — became the norm
    rather than the exception. Ludwig Göransson's reign from 2018 onward has introduced a new intensity
    of concentration that mirrors, and may yet exceed, the Golden Age dominance of Steiner and Tiomkin.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 6. MILESTONES ══════════════ -->
<section id="milestones">
  <h2><span class="sec-num">06</span> Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">1934</div>
      <div class="tl-title">First Winner: Louis Silvers — <em>One Night of Love</em></div>
      <div class="tl-desc">The very first recipient of the Best Original Score Oscar, establishing the category at the 6th Academy Awards. Silvers never won again — one of 55 single-win composers in the dataset.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">1950–1951</div>
      <div class="tl-title">First Back-to-Back Win: Franz Waxman</div>
      <div class="tl-desc"><em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> — the first (and for decades, one of only a few) consecutive wins by a single composer.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">1952–1954</div>
      <div class="tl-title">Three Wins in Seven Years: Dimitri Tiomkin's Golden Age Dominance</div>
      <div class="tl-desc">Tiomkin won in 1952, 1954, and 1958 — three wins in seven years set the benchmark for mid-century concentration that would stand until Alan Menken's run in the early 1990s.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">1980s</div>
      <div class="tl-title">Perfect Decade Diversity: 10 Unique Winners in 10 Ceremonies</div>
      <div class="tl-desc">The only decade in Oscar scoring history to produce a different winner every single year — a record that stands to this day and may never be broken.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">1989–1992</div>
      <div class="tl-title">Alan Menken's Animation Sweep</div>
      <div class="tl-desc">Three wins in four years (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>) during Disney's Second Golden Age. Menken is one of only two composers to win consecutively (1991–1992).</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">1966–1990</div>
      <div class="tl-title">John Barry's 24-Year Win Span</div>
      <div class="tl-desc">Barry's four wins span from 1966 (<em>Born Free</em>) to 1990 (<em>Dances with Wolves</em>) — a 24-year window that is the widest of any four-time winner. His last win came more than two decades after his first.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">2001–2003</div>
      <div class="tl-title">Howard Shore's <em>Lord of the Rings</em> Sweep</div>
      <div class="tl-desc">Shore won for both <em>Fellowship of the Ring</em> (2001) and <em>Return of the King</em> (2003), with a one-year gap. He became the rare composer to win for two films in the same franchise.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">2019</div>
      <div class="tl-title">First Woman to Win: Hildur Guðnadóttir — <em>Joker</em></div>
      <div class="tl-desc">A watershed moment in the category's 85-year history: Guðnadóttir became the first woman ever to win the Best Original Score Oscar, for her stark, cello-driven score for <em>Joker</em>.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">2018–2026</div>
      <div class="tl-title">Ludwig Göransson: Fastest Four-Win Accumulation in History</div>
      <div class="tl-desc">Four wins in eight years (<em>Black Panther</em>, <em>Oppenheimer</em> ×2, <em>Sinners</em>) — a pace of accumulation with no historical precedent. At the 2026 ceremony, Göransson joined Williams and Barry atop the all-time leaderboard while still in mid-career.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-year">2026</div>
      <div class="tl-title">Most Recent Winner: Ludwig Göransson — <em>Sinners</em></div>
      <div class="tl-desc">The 98th Academy Awards ceremony, March 2026. Göransson's fourth win for <em>Sinners</em> (directed by Ryan Coogler) cements his position as the defining composer of the contemporary era.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════ 7. CONCLUSIONS ══════════════ -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <h2><span class="sec-num">07</span> Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

  <ul class="takeaway-list">
    <li>
      <span class="tk-num">01</span>
      <span class="tk-text">
        <strong>Concentration is cyclical, not directional.</strong> The history of Oscar scoring alternates
        between eras of dominant personalities (1950s Tiomkin, early 1990s Menken, 2020s Göransson) and eras
        of wide-open competition (1970s, 1980s, 2010s). No single era has permanently resolved this tension —
        each concentration eventually gives way to renewed diversity.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tk-num">02</span>
      <span class="tk-text">
        <strong>Globalization has structurally changed the field.</strong> The Contemporary era (2000–2026)
        has the highest count of unique nationalities among winners: composers from Argentina, Iceland,
        Germany, India, Japan, and Sweden have all won, compared to the almost exclusively American and
        British cohort of earlier decades. This is not an anomaly — it reflects the Academy's evolving
        membership and the global economics of prestige cinema.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tk-num">03</span>
      <span class="tk-text">
        <strong>The 1980s remain the high-water mark of competitive diversity.</strong> Ten unique winners
        in ten ceremonies is a theoretical maximum, and the decade achieved it. The stylistic breadth of
        that decade — spanning Vangelis's electronics, Ennio Morricone's influence (though he did not win
        until 2015), jazz, and traditional orchestral work — has not been matched since.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tk-num">04</span>
      <span class="tk-text">
        <strong>The repeat-winner effect is concentrated among a small cohort.</strong> With a 25.8% repeat
        winner rate, roughly one in four ceremonies rewards a past winner — but that figure is driven almost
        entirely by the 14 composers with multiple wins. The remaining 55 single-win composers (79% of all
        winners) received exactly one career Oscar, suggesting the award rewards breakthrough moments as
        often as sustained dominance.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tk-num">05</span>
      <span class="tk-text">
        <strong>Göransson's trajectory is historically unprecedented and bears watching.</strong> If he
        continues at his current rate through 2030, he could stand alone at the top of the all-time leaderboard.
        However, historical precedent cautions against projecting continued dominance: no four-time winner
        has added a fifth Oscar. Whether Göransson breaks that pattern will be the defining storyline of
        Oscar scoring in the late 2020s.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

